WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.540
BEN WEISSMANN: UROP is
Undergraduate Research

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.980
Opportunities Program, and it's
basically just a fancy

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.210
word for undergraduate
research.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.500
So the idea is that
undergraduates can work with

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.295
grad students and professors
on their research.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.080
Now, it's having
lots of issues.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.290
It's being really about
recognizing--

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.990
YIHUI SAW: I started out at
MIT as a math major, but I

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:29.870
started dabbling a little
with programming.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.310
And I found that I was really
interested in it.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.882
And I was looking through some
of the projects at the Media Lab,

00:00:34.882 --> 00:00:39.020
and I found this really
awesome project that has that

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.300
sort of interaction with
technology that is the vision

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.940
in the future.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:47.090
BEN WEISSMANN: The basic idea is
to use computers to augment

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.310
physical spaces.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:51.000
Instead of just altering how
you perceive reality, it

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.820
actually changes reality by
projecting on top of it.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.720
YIHUI SAW: You have the
computer, the projector, and

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.170
the cameras built into one.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:00.700
BEN WEISSMANN: You take the idea
like the form factor of a

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.820
light bulb, and then instead
of having a filament, you

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.430
replace that with cameras
and projectors and

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:05.220
that sort of stuff.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.720
So it doesn't just shine light
down, it can see everything

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.220
and then react to that and
project different things down

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.150
instead of just plain light.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.130
RONY KUBAT: LuminAR brings
together electrical

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:17.460
engineering, mechanical
engineering, computer science.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.000
We've had UROPs in each of these
different disciplines.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.022
NATHAN LINDER: So if I don't
want to go to the database and

00:01:24.022 --> 00:01:25.700
edit or whatever--

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.145
YIHUI SAW: Yeah, like the most
recent week that we have on

00:01:28.145 --> 00:01:28.680
the database.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:29.720
NATHAN LINDER: OK, that's
a great solution.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.240
Let's just do that.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:32.400
The deal is very simple.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.160
It's a team and everybody gets
responsibility and gets thrown

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.890
to the deep, cold water
very quickly, and

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:39.430
they need to swim.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:42.980
It doesn't mean you let people
drown, but it means they need

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.130
to chart their course and teach

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:46.120
themselves. In the process

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.230
we learn a lot.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.750
YIHUI SAW: We could also
pull some from

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:51.210
the performance records.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:55.390
One of my roles in the project
is to explore what the system

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:59.560
can do. The Natura app was
really to demonstrate how

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.820
quickly you could develop
on the system.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.450
I was told Natura coming the day
before, and I just started

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.150
pulling information from
the website and

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.470
built it within a day.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:15.130
BEN WEISSMANN: You mark a region
that you're interested

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:18.890
in, which what you think of as
a button, and then it watches

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:22.180
your hand, and any time a hand
goes over that button it fires

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:25.080
off an event, and then we can
respond to those events.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.260
YIHUI SAW: You could project the
color of the makeup right

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.230
onto your skin itself and
compare to skin tone.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.220
BEN WEISSMANN: It's not so far
off from the future that it's

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.590
incomprehensible that we have
this in our homes in 5, 10

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:39.440
years, but it's still futuristic
enough that it's a

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.130
really, really cool
research topic.

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:46.130
RONY KUBAT: A lot of web
technologies are developing so

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:49.170
quickly that some of our UROPs
have come in, this is sort of

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.570
what they grew up as their first
languages, and so they

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:57.610
have a fluency with them
that we don't have yet.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.730
BEN WEISSMANN: One of the big
benefits of UROP is that it

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:02.540
gives you a lot of hands-on
experiences verses classes

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:03.980
where you have these very
structured projects.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.470
It's like, oh, you do this, and
then you get a grade on it

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:07.970
and it's either right
or wrong.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.680
And what's cool about research
is that you don't

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:11.880
have a right or wrong.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.820
You come up with an idea, and
you have a goal, and then you

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.400
work towards it and have
a product at the end.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.010
So you have something that you
can actually show for it

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.080
instead of some little
class project.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:24.560
YIHUI SAW: The research is
really cutting edge, and to be

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.400
involved could really help you
gain the skills and knowledge

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:32.060
that you don't get from a
classroom experience.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.760
RONY KUBAT: The project is too
big for any one or two person

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.730
to do on their own.

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:40.200
It would not have been possible
without our UROPs.

